nge 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7642505024961935},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-89","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"89","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine and Nonroad Engine Pollution Control Standards; The \"Omnibus\" Low NOX Regulation; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-17.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Federal preemption","Hybrid, electric, and advanced technology vehicles","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"O000019","cosponsorCount":32,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/89","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects emissions compliance obligations for manufacturers and operators of heavy-duty trucks, buses, and diesel-powered vehicles and equipment."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Vehicle and engine manufacturers would face different regulatory requirements depending on whether California's stricter emissions standards remain in effect or are preempted by federal rules."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Construction firms use off-road diesel equipment covered by the waiver, and the bill would affect compliance costs and equipment specifications for these machines."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Agricultural operations use off-road diesel engines and heavy-duty vehicles subject to the emissions standards that would be affected by revoking the waiver."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would cancel a waiver that allows California to enforce stricter emissions standards than federal rules for heavy-duty trucks, buses, and off-road diesel equipment starting with 2024 models. The waiver, granted by the Environmental Protection Agency under the Clean Air Act, permits California to set its own limits on nitrogen oxide pollution from these vehicles and engines. If passed, the resolution would restore federal preemption and prevent California from enforcing these particular state-level standards.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who drives. People living near a road, a plant or a port.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000806","score":0.05252904442813575},"overlapCount":32},"meaning":{"percentile":64.18993532102856,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7642505024961935}},{"billId":"119-hjres-87","bioguideId":"B001322","cycle":2026,"score":0.007672200441447248,"matches":[{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":24000,"donorShare":0.019969215789758767,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.006323585000090276},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.004160253289533077,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0006933755482555128},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":2625,"donorShare":0.002184132977004865,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0006552398931014595}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7642505024961935},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-87","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"87","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine Pollution Control Standards; Heavy-Duty Vehicle and Engine Emission Warranty and Maintenance Provisions; Advanced Clean Trucks; Zero Emission Airport Shuttle; Zero-Emission Power Train Certification; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-15.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Federal preemption","Legislative rules and procedure","Motor carriers","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000307","cosponsorCount":24,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/87","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects regulatory obligations for trucking companies and heavy-duty vehicle operators subject to California emission standards."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill would change compliance requirements for manufacturers of heavy-duty trucks and engines sold in California."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Revoking zero-emission truck mandates would reduce market requirements for electric vehicle charging infrastructure and clean powertrain technology."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Changes to zero-emission vehicle requirements could affect electricity demand and infrastructure planning for fleet electrification."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would cancel waivers that allow California to enforce its own stricter emission standards for heavy-duty trucks and engines. Under the Clean Air Act, California can request permission to set its own vehicle pollution rules that are tougher than federal standard